9
00:07:04,285 --> 00:07:10,285
The New Jersey Turnpike (NJTP) is a system of
controlled-access toll roads in the U.S.

10
00:07:51,428 --> 00:07:57,428
state of New Jersey.

11
00:08:38,571 --> 00:08:44,571
The turnpike is maintained by the New Jersey Turnpike Authority (NJTA).

12
00:09:25,714 --> 00:09:31,714
The 117.2-mile (188.6 km) mainline's southern terminus is at the Delaware
Memorial Bridge on Interstate 295 (I-295) in Pennsville Township.

13
00:10:12,857 --> 00:10:18,857
Its northern terminus is at an interchange with U.S.

14
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:06,000
Route 46 (US 46) in Ridgefield Park.

15
00:11:47,142 --> 00:11:53,142
Construction of the mainline, from concept to completion, took
a total of 22 months between 1950 and 1951.

16
00:12:34,285 --> 00:12:40,285
It was opened to traffic on November 5, 1951, between
its southern terminus and exit 10.

17
00:13:21,428 --> 00:13:27,428
The turnpike is a major thoroughfare providing access to various localities in New Jersey, and the toll road provides a
direct bypass southeast of Philadelphia for long-distance travelers between New York City and Washington, D.C.

18
00:14:08,571 --> 00:14:14,571
According to the International Bridge, Tunnel and Turnpike Association, the turnpike is the nation's
sixth-busiest toll road, and one of the most heavily traveled highways in the nation.

19
00:14:55,714 --> 00:15:01,714
The northern part of the mainline turnpike, along with the entirety of its extensions and spurs, is a part of the Interstate
Highway System designated as I-95 between exit 6 in Mansfield Township, and its northern end near New York City.

20
00:15:42,857 --> 00:15:48,857
South of exit 6, it has the unsigned Route 700 designation.

21
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:36,000
There are three extensions and two spurs, including the Newark Bay Extension at exit 14, which carries I-78; the Pennsylvania Turnpike Extension, officially known as the Pearl Harbor Memorial Turnpike Extension, at exit 6, which carries I-95 off
the mainline turnpike; the Eastern Spur and the Western Spur, which split traffic between Newark and Ridgefield; and the Interstate 95 Extension, which continues the mainline to the George Washington Bridge approach in Fort Lee.

22
00:17:17,142 --> 00:17:23,142
All segments (excluding the I-95 Extension) are toll roads.

23
00:18:04,285 --> 00:18:10,285
The route is divided into four roadways between exit 6 and exit 14.

24
00:18:51,428 --> 00:18:57,428
The inner lanes are generally restricted to cars, while the
outer lanes are open to cars, trucks, and buses.

25
00:19:38,571 --> 00:19:44,571
The turnpike has 12-foot-wide (3.7 m) lanes, 10-foot-wide (3.0 m) shoulders, and 13 of
the highway's service areas are named after notable New Jersey residents.

26
00:20:25,714 --> 00:20:31,714
The Interstate Highway System took some of its design
guidelines from those of the turnpike.

27
00:21:12,857 --> 00:21:18,857
The turnpike has been referenced many times in music, film, and television.
